@meta
id: 26-02
title: Filter a range, not the file
chapter: 26
type: lesson
skills: filter-bang
par: 21
minutes: 4
vscode: select the block, then hope an extension has a command for it
open: event-allowlist.md

@brief
The allow list in the middle of this document is a configuration block
inside a document that is mostly prose. Three keys got added twice. Sort
the block and drop the duplicates, and leave every other line — the
heading, the paragraph above, the note below — exactly as it is.

`:%` would have taken the prose with it and sorted the heading into the
middle of the keys. The block runs from line 6 to line 13.

@keys
| `:6,13!cmd` | filter lines 6 to 13 only |
| `sort -u` | sort and keep one of each duplicate line |
| `:.,+5!cmd` | the same idea with a relative range |

@hints
The range in front of the `!` is an ordinary ex range. Everything chapter
25 taught about addressing applies here unchanged.
--
`sort` has a `-u` flag that does what `:sort u` does — duplicates are
dropped after the sort, so the two copies always end up adjacent first.
--
`:6,13!sort -u` and Enter, then `:wq`.

@solution
    :6,13!sort -u<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Note what happened to the line count. Eight lines went in and five came
out, and vim did not mind in the slightest. A filter is not a
line-for-line transformation: the lines you selected are removed and the
command's output is inserted in their place. `grep` filters give you
fewer lines, `awk` can give you more, and both are ordinary uses.

That is also why the range matters more here than it does with `:s`. A
substitute with a range too wide might change a line you did not mean to
change. A *filter* with a range too wide feeds the wrong lines to a
program that will happily rewrite all of them.

Two habits keep this safe. Pick the range off the line numbers, or mark
the block and use `:'a,'b!cmd`. And when it goes wrong — it will — press
`u` once.

@start event-allowlist.md
# Webhook allow list

The gateway drops any event whose routing key is not in this block.
Keep it sorted, one key per line, no duplicates.

order.placed
payment.captured
order.placed
customer.created
payment.captured
order.shipped
customer.created
order.cancelled

Anything below this line is notes, not configuration.

@expected event-allowlist.md
# Webhook allow list

The gateway drops any event whose routing key is not in this block.
Keep it sorted, one key per line, no duplicates.

customer.created
order.cancelled
order.placed
order.shipped
payment.captured

Anything below this line is notes, not configuration.
